1
00:00:02,200 --> 00:00:04,481
So what is the problem with our graph here?

2
00:00:05,316 --> 00:00:06,842
Well, I'm not happy with the time

3
00:00:06,858 --> 00:00:07,710
complexity.

4
00:00:08,529 --> 00:00:11,003
We get various places where time complexity

5
00:00:11,340 --> 00:00:12,239
is a problem.

6
00:00:12,882 --> 00:00:15,516
For example, whenever we call indexOf,

7
00:00:16,319 --> 00:00:19,259
indexOf actually needs to visit all

8
00:00:19,934 --> 00:00:21,716
elements in the given array

9
00:00:22,439 --> 00:00:25,732
which we store for edge's start node to find

10
00:00:25,732 --> 00:00:28,689
out whether end node exists there. So this

11
00:00:28,786 --> 00:00:31,490
looks harmless, but this quickly is O

12
00:00:32,070 --> 00:00:33,760
for adding an edge, for example.

13
00:00:34,581 --> 00:00:37,141
For removing a node, we clearly have a

14
00:00:37,141 --> 00:00:39,892
problem with our nested loop here. So that

15
00:00:39,892 --> 00:00:41,303
might not be optimal like that.

16
00:00:43,086 --> 00:00:45,138
And therefore, it would be interesting to

17
00:00:45,138 --> 00:00:46,980
know whether we can do better.

18
00:00:47,707 --> 00:00:50,341
And it turns out that we can by using

19
00:00:50,777 --> 00:00:53,553
different JavaScript data structures. And

20
00:00:53,735 --> 00:00:56,041
actually, we can use data structures that

21
00:00:56,121 --> 00:00:56,864
are built in.

22
00:00:57,897 --> 00:01:00,415
Now we got our nodes and edges objects up

23
00:01:00,431 --> 00:01:00,609
there

24
00:01:01,303 --> 00:01:03,014
and we can stick to objects here

25
00:01:03,884 --> 00:01:07,234
but I wanna change how we store our edges.

26
00:01:07,589 --> 00:01:10,987
So the actual edges, the connections for a

27
00:01:11,052 --> 00:01:14,216
given node in this edges object thereafter.

28
00:01:16,239 --> 00:01:17,421
So for example here in

29
00:01:18,095 --> 00:01:18,325
addEdge,

30
00:01:19,163 --> 00:01:22,491
we do set our edges here to an array

31
00:01:23,278 --> 00:01:26,090
and we initialize it to be an array with the

32
00:01:26,090 --> 00:01:28,998
end node for which we add the edge if we had

33
00:01:28,998 --> 00:01:29,994
no edges before,

34
00:01:30,604 --> 00:01:32,773
otherwise we use push to add a new

35
00:01:32,773 --> 00:01:33,303
connection.

36
00:01:34,299 --> 00:01:35,858
Well, instead of using an array,

37
00:01:36,468 --> 00:01:37,995
I wanna use a set here now.

38
00:01:38,782 --> 00:01:40,967
Now to the set we can still pass an array

39
00:01:40,967 --> 00:01:43,344
with end node and that will automatically

40
00:01:43,682 --> 00:01:46,815
add end node as a value to this set.

41
00:01:47,556 --> 00:01:49,703
The special thing about a Set is

42
00:01:50,300 --> 00:01:54,562
that it is storing unique values only and it

43
00:01:54,562 --> 00:01:56,984
can do that very efficiently in memory.

44
00:01:59,300 --> 00:02:01,394
And therefore, this actually allows us to

45
00:02:01,427 --> 00:02:03,715
perform certain operations in a more

46
00:02:03,844 --> 00:02:05,858
efficient way in JavaScript.

47
00:02:06,692 --> 00:02:08,766
Now, when we add an edge to an already

48
00:02:08,782 --> 00:02:10,030
existing Set,

49
00:02:10,564 --> 00:02:13,740
we no longer push because push is not a

50
00:02:13,805 --> 00:02:14,323
thing on Set.

51
00:02:15,347 --> 00:02:16,753
Instead, on a Set,

52
00:02:17,724 --> 00:02:19,422
if we create one here,

53
00:02:20,457 --> 00:02:22,656
we have an add method instead.

54
00:02:23,760 --> 00:02:24,633
So we can just call

55
00:02:25,798 --> 00:02:26,315
add here

56
00:02:26,865 --> 00:02:28,774
and add our end node like that.

57
00:02:29,794 --> 00:02:32,812
And we also don't have indexOf to find out

58
00:02:32,812 --> 00:02:34,215
whether a value exists,

59
00:02:36,894 --> 00:02:37,588
but instead

60
00:02:38,186 --> 00:02:39,769
we have a has method.

61
00:02:41,065 --> 00:02:43,127
And the great thing about the has method

62
00:02:43,706 --> 00:02:45,220
is that it's super efficient.

63
00:02:45,977 --> 00:02:47,539
Now this doesn't return us an

64
00:02:48,135 --> 00:02:51,470
index, so we don't need to compare this. We

65
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:54,529
just check whether we already have an entry

66
00:02:54,871 --> 00:02:55,538
for end node.

67
00:02:57,669 --> 00:03:00,009
Or to be precise, since we wanna add that

68
00:03:00,009 --> 00:03:02,366
entry, we check whether we don't have that

69
00:03:02,366 --> 00:03:05,367
yet. So whether we do have an existing set,

70
00:03:05,674 --> 00:03:08,106
but in that set we don't hence the

71
00:03:08,106 --> 00:03:10,558
exclamation mark, have an entry for end node

72
00:03:10,558 --> 00:03:10,720
yet.

73
00:03:12,380 --> 00:03:13,560
This will use a set

74
00:03:14,175 --> 00:03:16,586
and has, unlike indexOf,

75
00:03:17,168 --> 00:03:19,239
has a time complexity of O.

76
00:03:21,067 --> 00:03:23,293
And that's of course great. With that we

77
00:03:23,293 --> 00:03:24,138
improved addEdge.

78
00:03:25,612 --> 00:03:29,391
Now for removing a node where we then also

79
00:03:29,795 --> 00:03:33,170
need to eventually find existing edges and

80
00:03:33,509 --> 00:03:34,139
remove those.

81
00:03:35,458 --> 00:03:36,619
Well, we of course still

82
00:03:37,231 --> 00:03:37,844
visit all

83
00:03:38,666 --> 00:03:39,617
edge entries

84
00:03:40,198 --> 00:03:41,391
in this edges,

85
00:03:42,148 --> 00:03:44,279
so there's no way around doing that

86
00:03:44,500 --> 00:03:45,245
unfortunately.

87
00:03:47,442 --> 00:03:49,767
But we can change this code a little bit.

88
00:03:50,751 --> 00:03:54,028
Instead of walking through all the stored

89
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:56,873
connections for a given node in this

90
00:03:57,527 --> 00:03:58,687
edges object,

91
00:03:59,259 --> 00:04:01,270
we can simply call this edges

92
00:04:01,886 --> 00:04:03,059
for the edge identifier.

93
00:04:03,911 --> 00:04:06,097
And keep in mind that this edges will be a

94
00:04:06,161 --> 00:04:06,435
set,

95
00:04:07,447 --> 00:04:08,042
and therefore

96
00:04:08,877 --> 00:04:10,953
call a method that exists on sets,

97
00:04:12,124 --> 00:04:13,572
the delete method.

98
00:04:14,954 --> 00:04:17,231
And delete takes a value we want to delete.

99
00:04:18,490 --> 00:04:18,875
So here

100
00:04:19,487 --> 00:04:22,317
we can simply call delete and forward the

101
00:04:22,317 --> 00:04:24,215
node identifier which we want to delete.

102
00:04:25,742 --> 00:04:28,139
And that's all. And that's also O.

103
00:04:29,356 --> 00:04:31,265
And we can get rid of all that code.

104
00:04:31,799 --> 00:04:35,083
So it's now way less code and also more

105
00:04:35,099 --> 00:04:36,005
efficient code.

106
00:04:36,652 --> 00:04:38,978
Now we still have that outer loop, but at

107
00:04:38,978 --> 00:04:41,218
least we got rid of the inner loop. Hence we

108
00:04:41,218 --> 00:04:43,152
achieved a better time complexity here.

109
00:04:44,720 --> 00:04:46,662
Now for removing an edge, here again I'm

110
00:04:46,759 --> 00:04:49,914
using indexOf. This does not exist on Sets.

111
00:04:50,319 --> 00:04:54,464
This should be has. And of course, therefore

112
00:04:54,464 --> 00:04:56,538
we no longer get back the node index.

113
00:04:57,044 --> 00:04:58,855
Instead, we can simply check whether

114
00:04:59,550 --> 00:05:02,494
we have a connection to that node here.

115
00:05:03,802 --> 00:05:05,972
And if this returns false, hence the

116
00:05:05,972 --> 00:05:08,013
exclamation mark here, we wanna throw this

117
00:05:08,094 --> 00:05:08,353
error.

118
00:05:11,022 --> 00:05:13,455
We could also merge this into one if check,

119
00:05:13,584 --> 00:05:15,807
by the way, by simply taking that

120
00:05:17,209 --> 00:05:20,045
and combining it with the OR operator here.

121
00:05:21,013 --> 00:05:23,774
With that, we check whether we don't have

122
00:05:23,774 --> 00:05:26,034
something for the start node or if we do,

123
00:05:26,750 --> 00:05:28,863
whether we maybe don't have an entry for the

124
00:05:28,944 --> 00:05:29,350
end node.

125
00:05:30,244 --> 00:05:32,194
And if we make it past this if check,

126
00:05:32,842 --> 00:05:34,568
we no longer use splice.

127
00:05:35,343 --> 00:05:35,956
Instead,

128
00:05:36,714 --> 00:05:40,216
we use delete to delete our end node here.

129
00:05:42,143 --> 00:05:42,912
Now for hasEdge,

130
00:05:44,350 --> 00:05:46,777
we still might wanna check if we got nothing

131
00:05:46,809 --> 00:05:49,301
at all maybe for the given start node, but

132
00:05:49,349 --> 00:05:52,435
thereafter it's not indexOf compared to some

133
00:05:52,500 --> 00:05:55,592
number, but it can just be has for the end

134
00:05:55,690 --> 00:05:55,917
node

135
00:05:56,825 --> 00:05:59,485
and that returns true if we have that entry

136
00:05:59,826 --> 00:06:01,188
and false otherwise.

137
00:06:05,834 --> 00:06:08,539
So that is how we could restructure this to

138
00:06:08,636 --> 00:06:12,001
use sets for storing our edge connections

139
00:06:12,194 --> 00:06:13,643
instead of an array.

140
00:06:15,011 --> 00:06:17,448
If we now reload this, we got the same

141
00:06:17,481 --> 00:06:19,818
result as before just with sets now,

142
00:06:20,604 --> 00:06:23,039
but we can clearly see that we got unchanged

143
00:06:23,087 --> 00:06:24,129
nodes, for example,

144
00:06:24,674 --> 00:06:26,581
but then sets with our connections.

145
00:06:27,414 --> 00:06:30,010
And if I don't remove that edge down there,

146
00:06:30,314 --> 00:06:31,612
so if I comment this out

147
00:06:32,366 --> 00:06:34,609
and we try that again, we of course get a

148
00:06:34,609 --> 00:06:35,378
graph where

149
00:06:36,372 --> 00:06:39,416
max has a set with 2 entries, for example.

150
00:06:40,250 --> 00:06:42,477
So that all seems to work. Let's also try

151
00:06:42,477 --> 00:06:44,945
